cooperation and containment that's what  the us president is said to want from  his chinese opposite number biden and xi  have been talking on the phone for the  first time since march after russia  invaded ukraine beijing has failed to  denounce moscow's war instead it's  threatened taiwan with invasion china  considers it part of its territory the  island governs itself democratically  biden has offered to come to its defense  if she tries reuniting taiwan with the  mainland u.s house speaker nancy pelosi  is said to be planning a visit to the  island beijing has warned of  consequences if such a high-profile  visit takes place it's important for us  to show support  mishanna kufner is following the story  for us from washington  it sounds easy just picking up the phone  but i don't think it's going to be that  easy for both leaders is it  well but they certainly have a lot to  talk about but these are testing times  the leadership of both joe biden here  facing economic data that's pointing  further and further towards a potential  recession here and xi jinping who in  october or november will have to face a  people's assembly there is no viable  democracy in china yet at the same time  that is an event where xi jinping could  see himself either strengthened or  weakened so both leaders need to ooze  power coming out of this telephone  conversation we're still waiting any  minute now for an advisory on what could  have been discussed because usually the  white house sends a readout but that  hasn't arrived yet so they may well  still be talking  biden's been especially unpopular with  beijing though hasn't he with  his his calls for her promises to defend  taiwan  yes he answered yes when he was asked  whether he would defend taiwan  potentially militarily the united states  have committed themselves to military  support also legally at the same time  they don't recognize taiwan as an  independent state and do pursue  something  called one china policy as well so uh  where does that leave both sides well in  a potential standoff as we are observing  china um exercising more and more power  and demonstrating more power in the  south china sea this all already came to  a head in the mid 90s which in the end  saw china actually sending rockets into  the sea  off the shores of taiwan now we're  nowhere near that but that visit of  nancy pelosi the speaker of the house  who can completely independently take  decisions of the president although she  is from the same party that could well  escalate tensions and if she backed down  and decided not to go that could portray  the united states as weakened so a very  difficult diplomatic territory both  between the two countries but also  amongst the democrats here who do face  midterm elections this fall and mikhail  i stress the word containment earlier  the u.s has said that china needs to be  contained where does that fit into this  conversation  well let's look at the economics it's it  is yes china trying to expand its power  in the south china sea and further  afield through its belt and road policy  um the us leading an initiative to  oppose that  in investment initiative in  within the g20 but also just having  passed a bill to invest hundreds of  billions of dollars into chip production  here in the us and that is so vital  because supply lines recently have not  just been hampered  by the pandemic but particularly by chip  production not keeping up and the main  exporter of those chips is indeed taiwan  and that's why it makes it a jewel in  the crown when it comes to international  economic power you heard it from  michelle now our correspondent in  washington thank you very much  and let's bring in ian chong an  associate professor at the national  university of singapore who focuses on  chinese foreign policy thanks for being  on the show today what do you expect  from this call between biden and she  honestly uh i expect quite a lot of  bluster and positioning but i really do  not expect any resolution to the  differences because the  issues at hand are very much intertwined  with domestic issues in both china the  prc and also the us so i don't think  leaders on the leaders on either side  have very much room for new maneuver  well uh with this crucial election  coming up for xi jinping i i bet there's  a lot of uh  well i wouldn't say acting but  a lot of playing to uh to the public  going on uh especially this anger over  nancy pelosi's proposed visit to taiwan  wouldn't you say  so um on the xi side it's not that he's  going for election i think it's pretty  clear he has his third term it's about  him being able to uh push his agenda  through being able to put his key allies  into important positions and uh the way  that she has positioned himself as the  leader of china is one that you know  focuses a lot on ethno-nationalism on uh  bringing up china's sort of national  sentiment uh sometimes in rather sort of  ethnic terms and  that can come out as pretty strident so  what that creates is a dynamic in the  prc where everyone has an incentive to  look strong no one has an incentive to  look weak uh least of all she so  i think uh there's going to be a lot of  forcefulness coming from xi and  certainly we see that on his rhetoric on  taiwan there's this new thing that he's  putting forward about how the taiwan  strait isn't international waters where  previously the the prc had not raised uh  these sorts of concerns so there's that  moving of the needle i think to position  she in a stronger position uh that  creates more friction not only with  taiwan but the u.s and u.s allies  talking about looking strong uh taiwan's  holding its biggest ever military drill  right now uh are the concerns of an  invasion real  so taiwan has had to deal with the  potential of her invasion for a really  long time um in the in the  late 90s early 2000s there was a view  that perhaps the economics would be more  important that certainly hasn't played  out so with the invasion of ukraine by  russia i think many people in town start  realizing that war could be quite real  so there's a lot more seriousness put  into the  current hang 138  exercise but it's not just the military  side there's a big civil defense element  that has seen far more uh involvement  from the public than was in the past so  this uh on taiwan's side there's  preparedness but i think they're also  trying to send china a signal that any  attempted invasion will be costly  lastly what sort of a chance of  of us getting any sort of cooperation  from these two leaders when when both  sides would like to see the other  contained  well i think for the us they've been  trying to talk about constrainment not  so much about containment the chinese  side has talked about containment  has accused the us of trying to contain  them now there is a lot to cooperate  over the the economy uh the continuing  pandemic  climate change but unfortunately i think  the politics on the two sides and the  mutual suspicions are so deep and also  the need for china to push this  nationalist narrative is so ingrained  that i think they that sort of very  useful and reasonable cooperation might  unfortunately have to be forgone  ian chong from the national university  of singapore thank you very much for  getting your take on this  thank you  let's turn to an expert on china and its  interactions with taiwan mariko ulbac  from the german marshall front think  tank  if you look at the drills there in  taiwan in moscow's invasion on ukraine  is it now really more likely that she  will take a page out of putin's book and  move on taiwan  i think it's definitely what's  definitely the case is that everybody is  watching very closely what's happening  in ukraine whether that is the chinese  side or the taiwanese side or the u.s  side that doesn't mean that there is you  know an imminent chinese invasion of  taiwan but everybody is studying what's  happening in ukraine and it's kind of  adjusting their plans accordingly and i  think they're right to take it seriously  now biden and shia are talking on the  phone today uh does taiwan need to be a  a topic there  i think taiwan will inevitably be a  topic i mean this phone call was set up  a while ago so i think initially the  plan was to talk you know about various  issues ukraine economy tariffs and those  things are still going to come up what  but with um nancy pelosi's planned visit  to taiwan and and the tensions around  that i would be very surprised if xi  jinping and joe biden decided to ignore  that topic altogether  now we've touched upon the topic earlier  what might china have learned from the  international reaction to the invasion  of ukraine  um i think on the one hand they've  definitely  learned that the costs may be higher  than they expected i do think that the  chinese side was taken aback  by the response um in europe and the  united states that there was a fairly or  a much more resolute response than they  had expected so i do think that does ca  that's something they're taking into  account um and i think this is also why  it's important to signal strongly  towards  china that the economic repercussions of  any any military steps in taiwan would  be quite severe  now  who has the upper hand at the moment in  this rather complicated relationship  sheobad  um i mean this is like a really hard  question to say i don't think it's  necessarily a question of of an upper  hand or not they both  have issues to deal with they both have  severe constraints and um i i do hope  that both sides still have some interest  in not letting it come to the worst um  so this is not an easy issue where you  could say x or  x or y has the upper hand  but i call back there from the german  martial fund think tank thank you very  much  thanks for having me
 

